### 670 -- Four Groups, Seventeen Base-Ten Representations

Input binding: /exercises/5; g0; p0221#6.
Task: express EVERY given positive number as a power with base 10,
using the graph if needed. Exact/approximate equality must remain distinct.
Define t,s,u,w as the UNIQUE real exponents satisfying respectively
10^t=7, 10^s=3.8, 10^u=5.2, 10^w=2.
This inverse definition needs no newly taught logarithm method.
The notation log10 in the verification appendix is only a mathematical shorthand.

a source: 1,10,10000,.1,.001.
Exact tuple of representations:
1=10^0; 10=10^1; 10000=10^4; .1=10^-1; .001=10^-3.

b source: 7,70,.7,.007.
Exact: 7=10^t; 70=10^(t+1); .7=10^(t-1); .007=10^(t-3).
From y=7 on the original graph, t is about .85.
Approximate exponent tuple: (.85,1.85,-.15,-2.15).
Thus the given numbers are APPROXIMATELY 10 raised to these rounded exponents;
they are not exactly equal to powers with these terminating decimal exponents.

v source: 3.8,380,.38,.038.
Exact: 3.8=10^s; 380=10^(s+2); .38=10^(s-1); .038=10^(s-2).
From y=3.8 on the original graph, s is about .58.
Approximate exponent tuple: (.58,2.58,-.42,-1.42).
The shift for 380 is +2, not +1.

g source: 5.2,200,.52,.0052.
Exact: 5.2=10^u; 200=10^(2+w); .52=10^(u-1); .0052=10^(u-3).
From y=5.2, u is about .72; separately from y=2, w is about .3.
Approximate exponent tuple: (.72,2.3,-.28,-2.28).
200 is SOURCE 200, not 520 and not 5.2 times an integral power of ten.
Its exponent is NOT u+2: that would give 520. The equation 10^(2+w)=200
uses the independent mantissa 2. A pattern mismatch is not a mathematical
inconsistency, not missing data, and not permission to create an erratum.

### 671 -- Six Exact Radical-to-Power Conversions

Input binding: /exercises/6; g1; p0221#8.
All radicands are positive; all displayed radicals denote their positive roots.

| Subpart | Full source input | Independent derivation | Exact answer |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| a | sqrt(10) | positive square root of 10 | 10^(1/2) |
| b | cube-root(100) | (10^2)^(1/3) | 10^(2/3) |
| v | 10*sqrt(10) | 10^1*10^(1/2) | 10^(3/2) |
| g | .1*sqrt(.1) | 10^-1*(10^-1)^(1/2) | 10^(-3/2) |
| d | .1*cube-root(10) | 10^-1*10^(1/3) | 10^(-2/3) |
| e | 100*fourth-root(10^-3) | 10^2*(10^-3)^(1/4) | 10^(5/4) |

In e the fourth root covers the complete 10^-3. Neither fourth-root(10)
followed by an external -3 exponent nor a negative fourth root is introduced.

### 672 -- Six Equations, Complete Real Solution Sets

Input binding: /exercises/7; g1; p0221#9 and p0222#1.
All six equations have domain R by the positive-base/radicand rules.
Use injectivity of 10^z, not a restriction to the plotted interval.

a source: 10^(1-x^2)=1.
1=10^0; hence 1-x^2=0, x^2=1, x=-1 or x=1.
Solution set {-1,1}; both substitute to 1. Unlike an even radical notation,
solving x^2=1 requires BOTH real signs. No other roots exist.

b source: sqrt(100^x)=.1.
For every real x, 100^x=10^(2x)>0 and its principal square root is 10^x.
Thus 10^x=10^-1 and x=-1. Solution set {-1}.
At x=-1 the original left side is sqrt(.01)=.1. No plus/minus branch,
no extraneous x=1, and no x>=0 domain restriction.

v source: (.01)^x=1000.
.01=10^-2 and 1000=10^3; so -2x=3.
Solution set {-3/2}; substitution gives .01^(-3/2)=1000.

g source: 10^(3-2x)=10^(x^2).
Right exponent is x^2, not x, 2x, or a new figure-derived value.
Injectivity gives 3-2x=x^2; x^2+2x-3=(x+3)(x-1)=0.
Solution set {-3,1}. At -3 both sides are 10^9; at 1 both are 10^1.
The factorization proves these are ALL roots; -3 is not rejected because
the illustrative source graph shows only nonnegative x.

d source: (.01)^x=cube-root(10).
10^(-2x)=10^(1/3); -2x=1/3.
Solution set {-1/6}.

e source: 1000^x=fifth-root(100).
The source root index is FIVE. 1000^x=10^(3x), fifth-root(100)=10^(2/5).
Thus 3x=2/5. Solution set {2/15}.

### 673 -- Six Inequalities, Quantifiers and Endpoints

Input binding: /exercises/8; g1; p0222#2.
All unknowns range over R. Interval notation refers to the COMPLETE real
solution set, not just plotted x. Infinity is never a included endpoint.

| Subpart | Full source inequality | Equivalent exponent inequalities | Complete solution set |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| a | (.1)^x > 10 | 10^-x > 10^1; -x>1 | (-infinity,-1) |
| b | 10^(x+1) < 10000 | x+1<4 | (-infinity,3) |
| v | 10^(x-2) > .01 | x-2>-2 | (0,infinity) |
| g | .1 <= 10^x <= 10000 | -1 <= x <= 4 | [-1,4] |
| d | 1 < 10^(x+1) < 10000 | 0<x+1<4 | (-1,3) |
| e | .01 < 100^x < sqrt(10) | -2<2x<1/2 | (-1,1/4) |

Endpoint verification:
- a at x=-1 gives equality 10, excluded; x<-1 alone makes the value greater.
- b at x=3 gives equality 10000, excluded.
- v at x=0 gives equality .01, excluded.
- g has source \leqslant on BOTH sides: x=-1 gives .1 and x=4 gives 10000;
  BOTH endpoints included. All x between satisfy both bounds; all outside fail.
- d at -1 gives 1 and at 3 gives 10000; both excluded by strict signs.
- e at -1 gives .01 and at 1/4 gives 100^(1/4)=sqrt(10); both excluded.
  The source base is 100, so the upper bound is 1/4, NOT 1/2.
These equivalences also prove completeness for every real x, not just sample tests.

### 674 -- Two Equations and Two Inequalities

Input binding: /exercises/9; g1; p0222#3.
All positive-base expressions have domain R.

a source: 8^(x-3)=16.
8=2^3 and 16=2^4, so 2^(3x-9)=2^4.
3x-9=4, x=13/3. Complete solution set {13/3}.

b source: 27^(2x-1)=9.
27=3^3 and 9=3^2, so 3^(6x-3)=3^2.
6x-3=2, x=5/6. Complete solution set {5/6}.

v source: 12^x < cube-root(144).
144=12^2>0; its positive cube root is 12^(2/3).
Since 12>1, the equivalent inequality is x<2/3.
Complete solution set (-infinity,2/3); equality at 2/3 is excluded.

g source: (.4)^x < 15 5/8, with a MIXED NUMBER on the right.
15 5/8=15+5/8=125/8=(5/2)^3=(2/5)^-3.
.4=2/5, so the inequality is (2/5)^x < (2/5)^-3.
The base is strictly between 0 and 1, hence the order REVERSES: x>-3.
Complete solution set (-3,infinity). At -3 the value is 125/8, excluded;
x=0 gives 1<125/8 and x=-4 gives 625/16>125/8, confirming the direction.
The source RHS is neither 15*5/8 nor a misplaced product or new source value.

